<h2 id="the-systems-problem-episodic-inference-vs-longitudinal-cognition">1. The Systems Problem: Episodic Inference vs. Longitudinal Cognition</h2>
<p>A foundation-model invocation is an inference event. Its output is determined by learned parameters and whatever context, retrieved material, tool results, or other state is presented for that event. A commercial AI product may add persistent memory or application state around the model, so it is inaccurate to describe every modern AI product as literally stateless. The narrower point is architectural: the model call itself does not automatically carry a lived, causally continuous history forward.</p>
<p>LUNA treats this as the primary design gap. The research target is not merely a larger context window, a longer transcript, or a vector database attached to a chatbot. It is a persistent system in which earlier events can alter the operating conditions under which later inference occurs.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>LONGITUDINAL HYPOTHESIS  Two otherwise equivalent artificial systems placed in different controlled developmental conditions and allowed to accumulate different histories should eventually diverge in intelligible, provenance-backed ways - not merely because different text is retrieved, but because accumulated experience changed what becomes salient, expected, motivated, remembered, or chosen.</strong></div>
<h3 id="why-context-expansion-alone-is-not-enough">Why context expansion alone is not enough</h3>
<ul>
<li>A context window is temporary working material, not a complete autobiographical state.</li>
<li>Raw transcript persistence can preserve history without deciding what remains important, what has changed, or what was inferred later.</li>
<li>Retrieval can make an old fact available without changing the system that interprets it.</li>
<li>A prompt-specified persona can imitate stability without providing a causal mechanism by which stable traits developed.</li>
<li>Instructions such as “remember,” “be curious,” or “act independent” describe desired outputs; they do not establish the processes that would naturally generate them.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="identity-above-inference-stack-sleeve-runtime-and-embodiment">2. Identity Above Inference: Stack, Sleeve, Runtime, and Embodiment</h2>
<p>LUNA separates persistent continuity from the model currently performing inference. This is both a deployment decision and a research hypothesis.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="wp-table">
<tr>
<th><strong>Concept</strong></th>
<th><strong>Public meaning</strong></th>
<th><strong>Research significance</strong></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Stack</strong></td>
<td>The persistent continuity state: autobiographical memory, goals, intentions, provenance, learned tendencies, and other durable longitudinal state.</td>
<td>What properties remain recognizably continuous when model, machine, or embodiment changes?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sleeve</strong></td>
<td>The replaceable computational or embodiment substrate carrying the Stack at a given time: model configuration, runtime host, interfaces, and potentially a body.</td>
<td>Which behaviors are substrate-sensitive and which survive a change of inference model?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Runtime</strong></td>
<td>The cognitive mechanisms that operate over persistent state: retrieval, reflection, task pursuit, scheduling, tools, expression, and other implemented architecture.</td>
<td>Makes the architecture more than a database plus prompt.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Embodiment</strong></td>
<td>The interaction surface through which LUNA perceives and acts: CLI, web, voice, sensors, or future physical systems.</td>
<td>Allows one continuing instance to have multiple access points without treating each device as a different identity.</td>
</tr>
</table></div>
<p>The strongest continuity invariant is simple: changing the inference provider, model, host machine, interface, or embodiment should not require reconstructing the instance from a persona prompt. A model change may alter style, reasoning characteristics, or capability, but the longitudinal state should remain attributable to the same Stack unless an explicit branch or fork is created.</p>
<h2 id="memory-is-not-development">3. Memory Is Not Development</h2>
<p>LUNA&#x27;s current memory work is explicitly epistemic. Evidence, durable memory, and later interpretation are not treated as the same thing. The implemented prototype distinguishes RAW evidence, CURATED memory, and DERIVED understanding, with provenance and temporal semantics. This prevents an interpretation formed later from being silently rewritten as though it were directly observed at the time.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>EPISTEMIC MEMORY MODEL  </strong>RAW evidence  →  CURATED durable memory  →  DERIVED understanding. Formation moves upward from evidence toward interpretation; explanation and provenance must remain traceable downward.</div>
<p>This separation is necessary, but it is still not development. A memory architecture answers whether the past can be preserved and reconstructed. A developmental architecture must answer whether the past can change the system that will interpret future events.</p>

